# Keep the build context to what the build actually needs.
#
# Without this, `COPY . .` uploads the entire working tree to Fly's remote
# builder on every deploy — .git alone is 136 MB of history that the compiler
# has no use for. It does not reach the final image (that is COPY --from=builder,
# a binary and one config file), but it is transferred every time and lives in
# the builder layer.
#
# The second reason matters more than the first. A build context is a copy of
# your repository handed to a remote machine: .git carries every version of
# every file ever committed, including anything added and later removed.

.git
.gitignore
.github

# Local configuration. .env is the one that must never be in a build context —
# an image is far more widely readable than a developer's laptop.
.env
.env.*
*.local

# Build output and caches — rebuilt inside the image anyway.
bin/
dist/
tmp/
*.test
*.out
coverage.*

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Documentation and scratch work, none of which compiles.
*.md
docs/
scratchpad/
